WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.130
From the implications of immortality-granting
whale goop to Free Willy tributes, Avatar:

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:09.221
The Way of Water has an ocean of small details
you might have missed.

00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:14.509
Most of James Cameron's previous films have
an anti-capitalist, or at least anti-corporate

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:15.902
stance within the film's narrative.

00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:19.950
In the previous "Avatar" film, this was shown
through the banal – but nonetheless coldly

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:24.619
malicious – machinations of the Resources
Development Administration, or RDA, as they

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:29.179
tried to kill off all the Na'vi "hostiles"
and hollow out the natural resources of Pandora

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:33.540
by enlisting a paramilitary unit, led by Stephen
Lang's Colonel Miles Quaritch.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.800
In "Avatar: The Way of Water", Colonel Quaritch
returns as the sequel film's main antagonist

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.800
once again.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.930
The film explains away Quaritch's resurrection
by stating that RDA had stored his memories

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.000
onto a disk prior to the first film's mission,
in case he were to die.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.489
And, since he did die at the end of the first
film, his memories were put into a Na'vi avatar

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.020
to return to Pandora and finish the mission
he started years later.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.520
"They could fix me up if I rotated back.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.090
Make me pretty again."

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:05.900
This brings up another example of the evils
of untethered capitalism and corporate power

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.750
in this dystopian future: you still have to
work for the company, even after you die.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:16.360
In "Avatar: The Way of Water," Quaritch, now
a Na'vi, doesn't seem too disturbed by this

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.670
revelation himself.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.130
However, the existential dread of how bad
the labor conditions on Earth have to be to

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.692
allow infinite forced employment is pretty
horrifying.

00:01:26.327 --> 00:01:30.350
At one point, the newly-resurrected Na'vi
clone version of Colonel Quaritch returns

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.920
to the place where Jake Sully and Neytiri
had killed him in his original human form

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.329
at the end of the first "Avatar" film.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:41.470
Since many years have passed since his death,
Quaritch's human body has now decomposed into

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.080
a rotting skeleton.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.970
Na'vi-Quaritch then picks up his dead human
skull, in an obvious reference to the "Alas,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.260
poor Yorick" monologue from "Hamlet."

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.780
This isn't just a simple visual allusion either.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.110
The speech is about the existential dread
of one's inevitable death, as well as the

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.280
futility of life itself.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.130
Hamlet had fond memories of Yorick, and as
he holds Yorick's skull in his hand, he laments

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.040
what he sees as a waste of life.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.440
So, too, does Quaritch as he holds his own
human head in his now-Na'vi hands.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.150
He is literally seeing the futility of his
past life, as well as the inevitability of

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.640
his own death, since it already happened.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.050
He attempts to come to terms with it, but
then decides to crush the skull in an act

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:29.230
of defiance to the blatant symbolism of fate
and the natural order of the life-death cycle.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.349
In the first "Avatar," actor Giovanni Ribisi
played the corporate villain Parker Selfridge,

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.790
who was the head of the Earth-based RDA that
was seeking Pandora's sacred unobtainium.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.640
He was essentially the main antagonist of
that film, alongside Stephen Lang's aforementioned

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.300
Colonel Quaritch – and in many ways was
worse than the Colonel.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.080
Selfridge himself represented the banal evils
of bureaucratic corporate greed.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.280
"Look, look, you're supposed to be winning
the hearts and minds of the natives.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.319
Isn't that the whole point of your little
puppet show?"

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:00.879
Ribisi's Selfridge is not a major character
in the sequel, but he does have a blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:02.390
cameo in it nonetheless.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.330
His appearance occurs towards the beginning
of the film, while Colonel Quaritch's recently-awakened

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.739
Na'vi clone watches a recording from his human-self
in the past.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:13.629
In the recording, Selfridge is seen in the
background, correcting Quaritch and detailing

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:16.417
the technology behind the memory-transfer
process.

00:03:16.845 --> 00:03:21.880
When James Cameron's original 2009 "Avatar"
film was first released in theaters, one of

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:26.670
the chief complaints was that it was basically
"Dances with Wolves," but with blue space

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.190
aliens instead of Native Americans.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.990
Both films are about a soldier from a colonizing
force "going Native" by falling in love with

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.640
the Indigenous people, and deciding to fight
his own kind to protect them.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.780
Furthermore, both films talk about how most
Native cultures would use the entire animal

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.250
after a hunt – utilizing the animal's meat
to eat, their hide to make clothes, their

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.980
bones to make tools, etc.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.680
"Avatar: The Way of Water," unsurprisingly,
has many breathtaking sequences in it.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.500
One of the most harrowing sequences in the
film is a chase scene on the Pandoran ocean

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.489
between a massive RDA hunting vessel and a
herd of sentient alien whale-like creatures

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:01.489
called "Tulkun."

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:07.330
It is an obvious allusion to the inhuman and
cruel whaling practices, as we see the RDA

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:12.659
hunting crew using their advanced sci-fi technology
to violently corner, trap, and kill an entire

00:04:12.659 --> 00:04:14.000
family of tulkun.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.629
It is also reminiscent of the way European
colonizers in the West would hunt animals

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:22.740
- like the buffalo - to near extinction for
their hides, while letting the corpses rot,

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.020
with no care for the wider ecosystem.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.480
Furthermore, while the giant tulkun are hunted
mercilessly, it's only so that the company

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:35.330
can extract just one, single vial of brain
goop – leaving the rest of the tulkun carcasses

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:36.330
to rot.

00:04:36.824 --> 00:04:40.960
What's interesting about "Avatar: The Way
of Water" is that, besides some off-hand mentions,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.140
the plot of the film is no longer predicated
on extracting unobtainium from Pandora anymore.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.990
Instead, the RDA is attempting to completely
terraform Pandora for human occupation, as

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:56.226
in the ensuing years since the first "Avatar"
film, it seems Earth has become completely uninhabitable.

00:04:56.226 --> 00:05:00.389
We don't spend a lot of time on Earth, but
it seems one of the main issues with the planet

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:03.139
in the "Avatar" universe is that it's overcrowded.

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:07.169
See, during the previously-mentioned tulkun
hunting scene, we discover the tulkun brain-goop

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:10.360
is used to completely stop aging in humans.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.820
This means that there is a population of people
on Earth who are essentially immortal – assuming

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.270
they can afford the goop.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:20.100
Adding to the fact that workers can seemingly
have their memories scanned to constantly

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.650
be re-cloned as an infinite labor force, it
seems the company's short-sighted, profit-motivated,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.540
and anti-labor policies are dooming Earth.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:33.229
This also goes against the balancing of energy
that the Na'vi follow on Pandora.

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:37.500
They say the Goddess Eywa gives energy for
life, but that it is only borrowed, and that

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.360
you end up giving the energy back in death.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.389
However, humans on Earth (presumably rich
humans) are trying to hoard their life - and

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:49.389
their energy - and not giving back it back
to nature, causing disruption, chaos, and

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:51.520
destruction on their planet in the process.

00:05:51.933 --> 00:05:57.110
It's no secret that "Avatar: The Way of Water"
director and co-writer James Cameron is obsessed

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:58.319
with the ocean.

00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:02.580
He's spent tons of money from his own personal
fortune to fund deep sea expeditions.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.419
So, it's no surprise that some of that design
aesthetic for the RDA's tulkun hunting operation

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:11.260
would have some aesthetic similarities to
real ships and subs.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:15.770
Even the more fantastical sci-fi designs – such
as the subs that have metallic crab-like legs

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:20.630
and mechanical claws – bear resemblances
to real tech in the field of deep sea diving.

00:06:21.144 --> 00:06:26.209
James Cameron's love for the ocean clearly
extends to the various aquatic life that inhabits it.

00:06:26.209 --> 00:06:30.380
This includes the whale-like tulkun, who live
in the ocean and visit the coastal villages

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.650
of the marine-based Metkayina Na'vi tribe.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:37.430
The Metkayina and the tulkun share a close,
sacred bond, as the tulkun are revealed to

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.870
be sentient creatures – arguably smarter
than both the humans and Na'vi – who are

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:45.600
even capable of complex language, mathematics,
and even art.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.540
At one point, Jake's younger son Lo’ak befriends
a wounded and "banished" tulkun.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.259
Throughout the rest of the film, Lo'ak and
the banished tulkun swim together and grow

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:56.680
a close and special bond themselves.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.819
The tulkun even becomes pivotal to the Na'vi
winning the final battle against the large

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:02.539
tulkun-hunting ship.

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:07.290
This subplot seems to be similar to the plot
of the 1993 family film "Free Willy," about

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:11.979
a young boy who befriends a mistreated orca
and then is able to free it from captivity.

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:16.039
Not only that, but there are seemingly overt
visual references to the film – such as

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:20.660
a slow-motion shot of it climatically jumping
in the air set right before Pandora's daily

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:25.180
eclipse that is very close to the visuals
of "Free Willy"'s cinematography.

00:07:25.576 --> 00:07:29.979
The climax of "Avatar: The Way of Water" is
set during a battle on top of the ocean, where

00:07:29.979 --> 00:07:34.180
the entire Sully family fights off Quaritch
and his recently Na'vi-ified Marine goons

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.700
on the massive deep sea tulkun-hunting vessel.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:40.639
Eventually, the raging battle creates enough
structural damage to the ship to cause it

00:07:40.639 --> 00:07:43.340
to flip upside down throughout the rest of
the scene.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:47.590
This, of course, lets the ocean water flood
the ship as it rapidly capsizes and sinks

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:50.050
– with the family stuck inside.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:54.390
During this exciting sequence, there are many
shots and situations that are very similar

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:58.360
to James Cameron's previous action-packed
epic set in the ocean, "Titanic."

00:07:59.076 --> 00:08:03.789
Speaking of James Cameron's '97 historical
blockbuster epic "Titanic," another returning

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:08.949
element from that film is the director casting
star Kate Winslet as the Metkayina Queen Ronal

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:10.699
in "Avatar: The Way of Water."

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:14.789
Cameron often likes to bring actors he's worked
with before on different projects, such as

00:08:14.789 --> 00:08:19.419
Bill Paxton appearing in "Terminator," "Aliens,"
"True Lies," and "Titanic," as well as working

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:23.360
on multiple films with star Arnold Schwarzenegger
and Sigourney Weaver.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.458
"I'll be back."

00:08:25.458 --> 00:08:29.460
However, there had been some tension between
director James Cameron and Winslet from their

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.709
turbulent time working together on the stressful
and disaster-prone "Titanic" set.

00:08:33.709 --> 00:08:37.649
Luckily, they've since been able to bury the
hatchet and finally work together again.

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:43.019
Meanwhile, Ronal is a strong and powerful
character, and she is the Metkayina who is

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:47.574
the most wary of helping and giving sanctuary
to Jake Sully and his family.

00:08:48.117 --> 00:08:52.280
James Cameron's "Avatar" series has often
been called a sci-fi version of revisionist

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.209
Westerns like "Dances with Wolves" and "At
Play In The Fields Of The Lord."

00:08:56.209 --> 00:09:00.519
All these films have a sympathetic view of
Native Americans and their struggles against

00:09:00.519 --> 00:09:05.339
violent and genocidal colonization from European
settlers, but they also usually show things

00:09:05.339 --> 00:09:08.260
from a white person's outside perspective.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:13.420
Even worse, they often fall into problematic
"white savior" and "noble savage" narrative

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:16.470
tropes, with "Avatar" unfortunately being
no exception.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:20.421
While "Avatar: The Way of Water'' doesn't
necessarily alleviate that problem all the

00:09:20.421 --> 00:09:25.610
way, the fact that Jake Sully has been fully-integrated
for years helps the narrative stay focused

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:28.649
solely on the Na'vi's own perspective at the
very least.

00:09:28.649 --> 00:09:32.640
This includes more backstory on the intricacies
of the politics between the various native

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:33.840
tribes of Pandora.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:38.220
For instance, there's initially immense tension
between Jake's family – who hail from the

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.470
forest Omaticaya clan – when they first
meet with the aquatic Metkayina tribe.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:47.410
It's true "Avatar" isn't entirely one-to-one
with real-life history, but this animosity

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:51.660
between the different Na'vi clans is reminiscent
of how many Native American tribes were warring

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:56.585
with each other, and had trouble working together
due to past tensions during the European invasion.

00:09:56.997 --> 00:10:02.220
The tension between the Omaticaya and Metkayina
seen in "Avatar: The Way of Water" is, unfortunately,

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:07.687
also due to the fact that - like humans - the
Na'vi can be biased against those physical differences.

00:10:07.687 --> 00:10:12.050
In "The Way of Water," the Metkayina Na'vi
have evolved, seemingly for thousands – if

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:16.601
not millions – of years to survive near
the oceans of Pandora, which means that their

00:10:16.601 --> 00:10:19.760
bodies have adapted specifically for that
environment.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.800
This includes thicker, turtle-like forearms,
longer and thicker tails, and stronger diaphragms

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:25.800
to swim better.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.839
Meanwhile, the forest-based Sully family are
more lithe and thin to make climbing and running

00:10:30.839 --> 00:10:32.180
through trees easier.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:36.779
Beyond those relatively obvious physical differences
between the forest and water Na'vi, another

00:10:36.779 --> 00:10:41.070
difference that exists - but is not really
commented on - is the difference in skin color

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:44.529
between two tribes, most likely due to the
different environments.

00:10:44.529 --> 00:10:49.170
The Metkayina's skin are essentially light
green, while the Sullys are a much darker

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:49.885
shade of blue.

00:10:49.885 --> 00:10:54.200
This adds an extra layer of racism and classism
to the movie's events.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.050
It also creates complications in mounting
a revolution, since past bigotries can make

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.860
it hard to bring disparate communities to
fight together against their common oppressors,

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:03.360
even when it's necessary.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.339
But as the film shows at the end, when King
Tonowari says that Jake and his family are

00:11:07.339 --> 00:11:12.485
now Metkayina after they fight together, banding
together and looking past bigotry is possible.

